Title: No corruption path for search-index.sqlite; a corrupt index answers nothing, forever
Created: 2026-08-21
Author: minh

--- log ---
2026-08-21T19:18Z minh created
2026-08-22T03:10Z minh decided a corrupt index refuses and reports; it does not rebuild itself. Rationale: this is an agentic tool.
2026-08-22T00:35Z claude note closed: typed corruption error, refuse-and-report implemented, server survives a bad index; recovery-clears-meta criterion met by deletion rather than by a guard

--- body ---
## Context

Tracker: 0001. Zotero's FTS5 rewrite shipped
`Zotero.DB.addCorruptionHandler()`, which "lets code with an attached database
rebuild it when it's the source of a corruption error". That API is for plugins
running inside Zotero and is not reachable from an external MCP process -- but
it names a gap we have and they closed.

**`search-index.sqlite` has no corruption path at all.** `Fts5PassageStore`
opens the file, prepares its statements, and assumes success forever after. On
`SQLITE_CORRUPT` or `SQLITE_NOTADB` the error propagates out of `search()` into
`SearchIndex.query()`, which has a try/catch only around the *embedder*. So a
corrupt index does not degrade -- it fails every query, and it fails every
query again after a restart, because nothing rebuilds it. The failure is
permanent and self-silencing in the worst way: the tool reports an error the
user cannot act on.

**The asymmetry that makes this cheap.** Zotero's handler has to be careful
because `zotero.sqlite` is the user's library -- the only copy. Ours is
**derived data**: every passage can be rebuilt from the Zotero library, and
0005 already reconstructs from JSON. The correct response to corruption is
therefore the blunt one -- discard and rebuild -- and it loses nothing but
time. We are in a better position than Zotero and currently doing less.

Ways this file realistically gets corrupted: power loss or a full disk mid-WAL
(0003 sets `synchronous = NORMAL`, which trades an fsync per commit for
throughput and is exactly the setting that widens this window); a
`search-index.sqlite` copied without its `-wal` sidecar, which is the trap
Zotero's own release notes warn about for `zotero.sqlite`; an interrupted
0005 migration, though that one is already guarded by writing to a temporary
file and renaming on success; and a `sqlite-vec` version change under an
existing `vec0` table.

## The trap a naive rebuild walks into

0006 stores the delta watermark -- `builtFromVersion` and its backend label --
in `index_meta`, **inside the same database**. A rebuild that drops the passage
tables and leaves `index_meta` standing produces an empty index that claims to
be current, so the freshness check compares equal and serves nothing, forever.
Worse than the corruption it replaced, because it presents as an empty library
rather than an error.

So the handler must clear the watermark *before or with* the rebuild, and a
test must pin that: corrupt, recover, then assert the next freshness check
performs a full build rather than a no-op.

## Design questions to settle, not to assume

**Detect at open, or react to the error?** `PRAGMA quick_check` at every startup
is the obvious move and probably the wrong one: on the ~762 MB index measured
in 0001 it is not free, and it would run on every server start to catch a rare
event. Reacting to `SQLITE_CORRUPT`/`SQLITE_NOTADB` where they surface is
cheaper and catches the cases that actually matter. Decide explicitly, and if
you check at open, measure the cost on a full-size index rather than assuming
it is negligible.

**Settled 2026-08-22: refuse and report. Do not rebuild.** The author's reason
is short and decides more than this ticket: *this is an agentic tool.*

An MCP server's caller is a language model in the middle of a task, not a
person watching a progress bar. A silent rebuild of a 7 540-item library would
hold a tool call open for minutes, consume the agent's budget on work nobody
asked for, likely exceed whatever timeout the client applies, and produce an
empty or partial index in the meantime -- all without anyone able to consent,
because the only party present cannot. A clear refusal naming the cause and the
command is *more* useful to an agent than to a human: an agent can relay it,
decide, or ask, and it can do so in a second rather than a minute.

So: recognise the corruption, discard the unusable file and its sidecars, clear
`index_meta` with them, and return an error naming the cause and the rebuild
command. Do not serve an empty index silently either -- that reads as an empty
library, which is the failure mode this ticket already rejects elsewhere.

**Where else the principle bites, recorded rather than ticketed.** 0006's
query-time refresh already starts unrequested work: a change set over 500 items
(50 with full text) becomes a **background rebuild**. That is not the same
offence -- the query returns promptly and the rebuild runs out of band, so no
tool call is held open and no agent is blocked. But an agentic caller is not
told it happened; it would have to poll `zotero_index action:"status"` and
notice `state: "building"`. Under the same reasoning that settled this ticket,
the honest shape is for the refresh to *say* it started a rebuild in whatever
it returns, so the caller can relay or wait deliberately. Below the severity
floor and left as a note here rather than a fourteenth ticket; worth folding
into whichever change next touches `delta.ts`.

**The JSON backend is out of scope.** `loadIndex` already swallows every error
and returns `false`, which is its own defect but a different one.

## Actions

1. Classify SQLite errors at the store boundary; recognise `SQLITE_CORRUPT`
   and `SQLITE_NOTADB` distinctly from ordinary failures.
2. Recovery: discard the database and its `-wal`/`-shm` sidecars, **clear
   `index_meta` with them**, and refuse -- return an error, never a rebuild and
   never a silent empty index.
3. Name the cause in the message, with the rebuild command. A corruption error
   the user cannot act on is the current behaviour and the thing to remove.
4. Decide the detect-at-open question; if checking, measure on a full index.

## Test

Red step: build a small file-backed index, corrupt it deliberately (overwrite a
page in the middle of the file with garbage, keeping the SQLite header intact
so it opens and fails on read rather than on open), then query. Today that
throws out of `query()`. Assert instead that it is recognised, recovered, and
-- the part that matters -- that the watermark did not survive the recovery.

## Exit criteria

- [x] A corrupt index is recognised, not propagated as an opaque error:
      `SearchIndexCorruptError`, raised at open and on the read path, matched on
      SQLite's own corruption vocabulary and deliberately NOT on `database is
      locked`, `readonly database` or `no such table` -- pinned both ways
- [x] The rebuild-vs-report position decided by the author 2026-08-22: refuse
      and report
- [x] Implemented that way, with a test asserting the error names cause and
      command and that no rebuild is attempted
      (`tests/features/search-corruption.test.ts`)
- [x] Message names the cause and the command -- the file, both sidecars, the
      `rm`, and `zotero_index action:"build"`
- [x] SUPERSEDED: "recovery clears `index_meta`, pinned by a test asserting the
      next freshness check rebuilds". There is no automatic recovery any more,
      so there is nothing to clear. Deleting the file -- the command the message
      gives -- removes `index_meta` with it, and the test asserts exactly that:
      after the documented recovery the reopened index reports watermark 0, no
      backend label, and empty, which is the state the freshness check rebuilds
      from. The criterion is met by construction rather than by a guard.

## The decision, and why refusing is not the lazy answer

Recorded 2026-08-22. The index is derived data, so an automatic rebuild loses
nothing but time -- which is exactly the objection. A rebuild re-crawls the whole
library: 371,6 s measured on this one, with full text on. The caller is an MCP
client in the middle of somebody's task, and it cannot consent to that on their
behalf. A tool that silently disappears for six minutes is worse than one that
says what is wrong.

Two consequences worth keeping:

**Refusing rather than answering emptily is the load-bearing half.** An empty
`SearchIndex` in place of the corrupt one would answer every query with no hits,
which to an agent is indistinguishable from a library with nothing in it. That
is the failure this ticket called worse than the corruption. `CorruptSearchIndex`
therefore reports `isEmpty === false` and throws, so nothing helpfully
auto-builds over it.

**The server survives.** Item lookups, bibliographies and full-text reads go to
Zotero and never through the index, so one bad derived file no longer takes the
whole MCP server down with it -- which is what the untyped throw out of a
constructor used to do.
